# Response headers for the static assets. Workers parses this file and never serves it.
#
# COMMENTS LIVE HERE, ABOVE THE RULE, NOT INSIDE IT. The documented form shows comments on
# their own lines outside a block; an indented "#" line under a URL pattern is not a header
# and was silently ignored along with the rest of the block when first written this way.
#
# WHY FRAMING IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS, AND IT IS NOT A SECURITY POINT. This site's product is
# the claim that a figure came from a named filing on a stated date. Without frame-ancestors,
# anyone may embed a research document in an iframe inside their own page, with their branding
# around it, and pass sourced work off as theirs. That is misattribution rather than attack.
#
# frame-ancestors ALONE RESTRICTS NOTHING ELSE. CSP directives are independent and there is no
# default-src here, so scripts, styles and fonts stay unrestricted. That is deliberate: the
# deliverables carry inline scripts for the light and dark toggle and load Google Fonts, and a
# stricter policy would silently break the toggle across all 36 documents.
#
# X-Frame-Options is superseded by frame-ancestors in current browsers and is kept for older
# agents that honour only it. Referrer-Policy is here because the documents link out to
# sec.gov, and without one the full URL of the page the reader was on travels with them.
#
# FOUR OTHERS WERE CONSIDERED AND REJECTED on 4 September 2026, so they are not re-proposed:
# X-Content-Type-Options guards MIME sniffing where every file already carries a correct type;
# Strict-Transport-Security closes only a browser's very first request, since Cloudflare
# already upgrades http; Permissions-Policy disables camera, microphone and geolocation that
# nothing here requests; and a full Content-Security-Policy is the one that breaks the toggle.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
